# Copy to deploy/deploy.env (gitignored) and fill in.
#
# The deploy target lives here rather than in the scripts because this is a
# PUBLIC repository. An origin host address is not a secret in the
# cryptographic sense — it is discoverable from DNS whenever the site is served
# directly — but publishing it has two concrete costs:
#
#   1. It defeats a CDN/proxy in front of the site. If the domain is ever
#      proxied for caching or DDoS protection, the whole protection rests on
#      attackers not knowing the origin address; a repo that prints it hands
#      that over permanently, including to anyone reading git history later.
#   2. It converts a general internet-background-noise target into a named one,
#      alongside whatever else that host runs.
#
# So: no host addresses, no co-tenant service names, no third-party ports in
# tracked files. The design *reasoning* stays in the comments — that a host is
# shared, that memory must be capped, that a port default collided — because
# that is what a future reader needs. The identifiers do not.

# SSH target for deploys, e.g. user@host or an ~/.ssh/config alias.
# An alias is preferable: it keeps the address in your SSH config, which is
# already the right place for it.
CVT_DEPLOY_HOST=

# Where the built site is served from, and where the pipeline checkout lives.
# Defaults are fine unless the host is laid out differently.
# CVT_DEPLOY_WEB=/var/www/chinovalley.today
# CVT_DEPLOY_APP=/srv/chino-valley-today
